===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[lion]][['s]] [[share]]|s|lions' shares}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} The [[majority]]; a [[large]] or [[generous]] [[portion#Noun|portion]].
#: {{synonyms|en|leonine share}}
#: {{ux|en|They got a large donation, but the '''lion’s share''' of the money went straight into paying off debt.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=David Brewster|authorlink=David Brewster|title=XXIX. ''Observations Connected with the Discovery of the Composition of Water.'' By Sir David Brewster, K.H., F.R.S., and V.P.R.S. Edin.|editors=David Brewster, [[w:Richard Taylor (editor)|Richard Taylor]], [[w:Richard Phillips (chemist)|Richard Phillips]], and [[w:Robert Kane (chemist)|Robert Kane]]|magazine=[[w:Philosophical Magazine|The London, Edinburgh and Dublin Philosophical Magazine and Journal of Science]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Richard Taylor (editor)|Richard and John E[dward] Taylor]],{{nb...|Red Lion Court, Fleet Street}}, printers and publishers to the {{w|University of London}}; sold by [[w:Longman|Longman, Brown, Green, and Longmans]] [''et al.'']|date=8 August 1845|year_published=September 1845|volume=XXVII|issue=179 (Third Series)|page=195|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hLU5mtc__HEC&pg=PA195|oclc=288956877|passage=In place of dividing the merit of the discovery between the Englishman and the Scotchman, and giving the '''lion's share''' to my countryman, ''I have given the whole merit of the discovery to'' [[w:Henry Cavendish|[''Henry''] ''Cavendish'']] ''the Englishman'', and have reserved only for [[w:James Watt|[James] Watt]] the Scotchman, the merit of the previous hypothesis,—a merit freely given him by Cavendish himself, and one which no other person ever claimed.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Women in the Middle Ages|magazine=[[w:Blackwood's Magazine|Blackwood’s Edinburgh Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Published by the Leonard Scott Publishing Company,{{nb...|140 Fulton St., between Broadway and Nassau St.}}|month=November|year=1867|volume=CII (American edition, volume LXV)|issue=DCXXV|page=631|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=_4AtAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA631|column=1|oclc=1042815524|passage=But when a fortunate section of them should begin to win '''lions' shares''' of the spoil through superior aptitude for the struggle, can we doubt in what direction the disappointed ones would seek for consolation and support?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Notes of the Week|journal=The Japan Weekly Mail. A Political, Commercial, and Literary Journal|location=Yokohama|publisher=[s.n.]|date=15 February 1873|volume=IV|issue=7|page=97|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6RlDAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA97|oclc=472426045|passage=But England suffered great damage, and was put to very heavy expense in compelling restitution. She voluntarily surrendered all claim to the '''lion's share''' of the prize, though she certainly did the '''lion's share''' of the work, and bore the '''lion's share''' of the expense.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Lee Meriwether|authorlink=Lee Meriwether (author)|chapter=Appendix|title=A Tramp Trip: How to See Europe on Fifty Cents a Day|location=New York, N.Y.; London|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]],{{nb...|Franklin Square.}}|year=1887|page=272|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/tramptriphowtose01meri/page/272/mode/1up|oclc=1474180|passage=The ''real'' cause of poverty in Europe is the great wealth of the few. The masses always be poor when a few kings and nobles hoard such enormous '''lion's shares'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Arnold Bennett|authorlink=Arnold Bennett|chapter=In the Universe|title=The Lion’s Share|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|George H. Doran Company}}|year=1916|page=346|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/lionsshare00benn/page/346/mode/1up|oclc=604386|passage=You would have the '''lion's share''' of everything. Now for myself I intend to have the '''lion's share'''. And why shouldn't I? Isn't it about time some woman had it? You can't have the '''lion's share''' if you are not free. I mean to be free. If I marry I shall want a husband that is not a prison&nbsp;…}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard Christie|author2=Florence Geis|chapter=The Ten Dollar Game|title=Studies in Machiavellianism|series=Social Psychology|location=New York, N.Y.; London|publisher={{w|Academy Press}}|year=1970|isbn=978-0-12-174450-2|passage=In fact, at the end of some of the Con Game sessions, some subjects suggested playing for money. We must add, however, that it was usually those who had collected  the '''lion's shares''' of the points who made such suggestions.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Les Roopanarine|title=[[w:Birmingham F.C.|Birmingham]] 1 – 0 [[w:Stoke City F.C.|Stoke]]|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9391008.stm|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180614101707/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9391008.stm|archivedate=14 June 2018|work={{w|BBC Sport}}|date=12 February 2011|passage=Yet it could have been so different for {{w|Tony Pulis}}'s side, who weathered a good start by the hosts to create the '''lion's share''' of what few first-half chances came along.}}

